y  shouldn't have been tried by the city  and also made the church unhappy as they  were the ones who were supposed to take  care of such problems since the  university was under the clergy's  protection  nonetheless such acts by the townspeople  prompted the university to suspend its  operation for a couple of years in  protest  some of the students and masters now  with no university and also fearing for  their life decided to leave oxford for a  newly emerging studium generally at  another city  the city being cambridge and these  people being the backbone for the  foundation of the university of  cambridge  so you could say that the townspeople of  oxford were indirectly responsible for  the foundation of a rival university  in 1214 the university with the help of  the church would reach an agreement with  the city  and open up operations once again this  agreement amongst other things mentioned  the official appointment of a chancellor  of the university  although there is no reason to believe  such position didn't exist prior to 1214  the first official holder of this  chancellory was robert grosatesta  maybe the sources are vague on this  point nonetheless we do know gracateste  was at least a master at the university  and was  instrumental in starting the oxford  intellectual movement  he pioneered the idea of controlled  experiments and demonstrative science  and at an age when alchemy was  older age he wrote books like  metaphysics of light  introductory text to astronomy  mathematical reasoning in natural  sciences  etc he also built much of the groundwork  on which roger bacon started off his  career  continuing on the university expanded  tremendously during the 13th century  first was the official recognition as  universitas in 1231 and the acquisition  of the royal charter 17 years later  the university started to be known all  around europe and the scholars came from  as far as hungary as the university  expanded in size complexity  faculty and student count the troubles  with the city started up yet again  although it is arguable if they ever  ended the large amounts of incoming  students became a problem as there  wasn't enough housing in the city  so this problem started to be solved by  housing students in  university-affiliated medieval halls  called aularian these medieval halls  don't exist anymore as they were  replaced by the college system  starting around the 14th century but the  last holdout to this day according to  them  is saint edmonds hall which states it is  the oldest  academical society for the education of  undergraduates in any university  now even though whole is part of its  name it is not a true medieval hall  anymore because it operates as a college  today and saint edmonds isn't the only  college at oxford that started off as a  whole so did university college  hertford college exeter college etc  going into the 14th century the town and  gown problem culminated in the battle  of the saint escalistic day or more  appropriately the saint scholastic day  riot  it started off where every good  disagreement starts and a tavern  two students thinking there was  something fishy with their drinks  confronted and then assaulted the  taverner which started a brawl between  the students and the locals  [Music]  after that event when the university  refused to punish the students the  townspeople  started a two-day riot and clashes  between the students and locals became  to resemble more of a battle than just a  riot  the townspeople attacked academic  housing with bows and arrows pitchforks  and swords and naturally the students  retaliated after two days of this the  fighting finally subsided but it left  63 students and 30 locals dead and many  more wounded  the church would later on take the  university side and punish the locals by  making the mayor and the councillors of  the city walked bare head to church on  every saint scholastic day for the next  500 years  with that said the university's 14th  century was highly productive the  university kept growing and  started to for the first time accept  tuition from incoming undergraduates  this meant that the university now  wasn't solely reliant on the endowment  of money from rich individuals the city  or the catholic church this created more  freedom of thought within the university  and may have been instrumental in the  continuation of the oxford intellectual  movement scholars that were part of this  movement were thinkers like simon breden  one of the earliest european  mathematicians to work on trigonometry  or dun scotus along with william of  alchem which are considered to be two  of the three most important philosopher  theologians of the high middle ages  and many more like john wickliffe who  translated the bible into english and  along with ochem was instrumental in the  foundation of the ideas which would  later on spur  up the protestant reformation in fact  martin luther himself would mention  allchem's ideas on several occasions  lastly we have to look at thomas  brodverden archbishop of canterbury  and a member of the oxford calculators  the oxford calculators who are mostly  part of the merton college  made numerous discoveries like the  difference between kinematics and  dynamics  theory of velocity and maybe most  importantly discovery of the law of  falling bodies  long before galileo was even born with  all that said when the last of oxford  calculators and john wycliffe died in  the 14th century they were considered  the last people of the oxford  intellectual movement  and with them the early oxford  intellectual renaissance ended  not to be seen again in such prominence  until the 18th and 19th century  why the oxford intellectual movement  ended so suddenly is usually accredited  to this small thing called the black  death  it came to england around the middle of  14th century but outbreaks continued all  the way till 1375. many oxford scholars  died to it  including brad verdin alchem breden etc  and the ones that survived weren't  enough to pass the intellectual torch to  the student body which was  also dwindling but just like a phoenix  university of oxford managed to rebound  from the ashes of the black death  through some very clever maneuvering  for one the city of oxford was also  devastated from the plague and the  university took this opportunity to buy  out large amounts of vacant land around  the city  for example this is how new college got  its start in 1379 when  vickim the bishop of winchester bought  up abandoned property near the city wall  the city gladly sold the land but had  one requirement which was that new  college had to take care and maintain  their part of the city wall  which they did to this day where it  still stands on the east side of the  college  this is where i will conclude my video  with the oxford intellectual movement  ending and the beginnings of what we  would recognize as the university of  oxford today  if you enjoyed the video please leave a  like or a comment and subscribe so you  don't miss a release of a very big  project coming out in november which  includes many big and small history  youtubers including  your truly m laser

